@geindex BinaryValue (pyGHDL.dom.Literal.DecimalBitStringLiteral property)
@anchor{pyGHDL/pyGHDL dom Literal pyGHDL dom Literal DecimalBitStringLiteral BinaryValue}@anchor{1780}
@deffn {Property} BinaryValue: str@footnote{https://docs.python.org/3/builtins/stdtypes.html#str}

Read-only property to return the literal’s string value, one character per bit (@ref{1781,,_binaryValue}).

The value follows IEEE Std 1076-2008, clause 15.8. It is computed from @ref{1782,,Value}, the base, @ref{1783,,Length}
and @ref{1784,,IsSigned} on first access and cached:


@enumerate 

@item 
Underscores are removed.

@item 
Expansion:


@itemize *

@item 
Binary, octal and hexadecimal: each digit valid for the base is replaced by its 1, 3 or 4 bits; hexadecimal
digits are case-insensitive. Each other graphic character, e.g. @code{X}, @code{Z} or @code{-}, is repeated 1, 3 or 4
times, keeping its case.

@item 
Decimal: the digits are converted to binary with the minimal number of bits (none for @code{0}).
@end itemize

@item 
With a length larger than the expanded value, the value is padded on the left: with @code{0} if unsigned or
unspecified, with the leftmost character if signed.

@item 
With a length smaller than the expanded value, leftmost characters are deleted: only @code{0} if unsigned or
unspecified, only copies of the leftmost remaining character if signed.
@end enumerate

Example: @code{12sx"F5"} → @code{111111110101}.

@*Returns: 
The literal’s string value, one character per bit.


@*Raises: 

@itemize *

@item 
@code{LiteralError} – If the value starts or ends with an underscore or contains two consecutive underscores.

@item 
@code{LiteralError} – If a decimal literal is signed or unsigned.

@item 
@code{LiteralError} – If a decimal literal contains a character other than a digit or an underscore.

@item 
@code{LiteralError} – If a digit is not allowed in the base, e.g. @code{2} in a binary literal.

@item 
@code{LiteralError} – If the value contains a non-graphic character.

@item 
@code{LiteralError} – If a signed literal with an empty expanded value has a non-zero length.

@item 
@code{LiteralError} – If the length of an unsigned or unspecified literal truncates a character other than
@code{0}. 
Only leading ‘0’ characters can be truncated.

@item 
@code{LiteralError} – If the length of a signed literal truncates a character other than the sign. 
Only copies of the leftmost remaining character can be truncated.
@end itemize

@end deffn

