@geindex RangeFromName (class in pyGHDL.dom.Range)
@anchor{pyGHDL/pyGHDL dom Range pyGHDL dom Range RangeFromName}@anchor{1ba5}
@deffn {Class} pyGHDL.dom.Range.RangeFromName (node, symbol)

This class implements a @ref{3,,pyGHDL.dom} object derived from pyVHDLModel.Base.RangeFromName@footnote{https://vhdl.github.io/pyVHDLModel/pyVHDLModel/pyVHDLModel.Base.html#pyVHDLModel.Base.RangeFromName}.

A range denoted by a name, so its bounds are inferred from whatever that name references.

The name is represented by a Symbol@footnote{https://vhdl.github.io/pyVHDLModel/pyVHDLModel/pyVHDLModel.Symbol.html#pyVHDLModel.Symbol.Symbol}, so the bounds become available once
that symbol is resolved. A constrained subtype indication keeps its type mark `and' its range
constraint, because it’s carried by a ConstrainedScalarSubtypeSymbol@footnote{https://vhdl.github.io/pyVHDLModel/pyVHDLModel/pyVHDLModel.Symbol.html#pyVHDLModel.Symbol.ConstrainedScalarSubtypeSymbol}.

@cartouche
@quotation Note 
Two forms reach this class, because a parser can’t tell them apart beyond “a name, optionally with
a range constraint”:


@itemize *

@item 
a range attribute like @code{vector'range}, and

@item 
a discrete subtype indication like @code{bit} or @code{integer range 0 to 7}.
@end itemize

VHDL’s grammar puts the latter one level up (@code{discrete_range ::= discrete_subtype_indication |
range}), so representing both as a range deviates from the rule split deliberately.
@end quotation
@end cartouche

